# crdt-model converge --runs 1000 --operations 10000 --threads 4   (seeds from 0xc0de000400010000, the default; commit f1331c4, release build; from 2026-10-08T16:22:13Z to 2026-10-09T01:39:10Z)
# Run in three parts, because the container that ran it restarted twice and stopped it each time: runs 0 to 473 with the command above (started 2026-10-08T16:22:13Z); runs 474 to 499 with --runs 526 --seed 0xc0de0004000101da (started 21:04:05Z, stopped by the second restart); runs 500 to 999 with --runs 500 --seed 0xc0de0004000101f4 (started 22:05:27Z). Every run depends only on its own seed, so together the parts are the run of the command above. The parts numbered their runs from their own first seed; here every run line is numbered from 0xc0de000400010000 and the lines are sorted by number. The third part's own summary follows them; the combined summary of all 1,000 runs at the end was computed afterwards from the run lines (each run counted once).
# Machine: 4 CPUs, Intel(R) Xeon(R) Processor @ 2.10GHz, 15 GiB RAM, Linux 6.18.44-fc-v80. The run shared the machine with the fuzzing run, the profiles, the gates, the confirmation run on the final code and replays of this run (results/README.md), so its run times are longer than on an idle machine; from 23:03 to 23:34 UTC on 2026-10-08 the third part ran at the lowest priority while a confirmation run had the machine, so the runs in flight then took up to 34 minutes (the slowest run time below).
# Operations refused with an adapter error (Crdt in the refusal counts): 87, in 75 runs. Each of those runs was replayed on an instrumented scratch build of f1331c4 that prints every such refusal; each replay reproduced its run's line below exactly, and every one of the 87 refusals was delete_column failing out of range after it had deleted cells of a row that the table's stored list held twice, with its deletions left pending for the next edit's undo step. The runs still converged. The final code fixes this (ea3f0ea) and fails a run as soon as an action leaves writes pending (af24393); report §2 and §3, AC-1.
